```

The `false` means has_null=false, so even offset=0 is treated as valid.

The sanitize for `LOffsetTo<T, false>` calls:
```cpp
inline bool sanitize (hb_sanitize_context_t *c, const void *base, Ts... ds) const
{
    TRACE_SANITIZE (this);
    if (unlikely (!c->check_struct (this))) return_trace (false);
    // has_null=false, so no null check
    if (unlikely (!c->check_range (base, (unsigned) *this))) return_trace (false);
    unsigned int offset = *this;
    const T &obj = StructAtOffset<T> (base, offset);
    return_trace (likely (obj.sanitize (c, ds...)) || c->edit (base, &offset));
}
```

Wait, I see something. The `check_range(base, (unsigned) *this)` checks that `base + offset` is within [start, end]. But it doesn't check that the OBJECT at that offset is within range - it only checks that `base + offset` is a valid starting point. The actual size check is done by `obj.sanitize(c, ds...)`.

But then `UnsizedOffsetListOf::sanitize(c, count)` does `sanitize_shallow(c, count)` which checks count entries, and then for each entry follows the offset. So all the lookups should be validated.

